title: "discipline: a referee for computational claims made with a language model"
type: software
version: "2.0.0"
date-released: "2026-11-07"
authors:
  - family-names: "Berenji"
    given-names: "Joseph"
repository-code: "https://github.com/josephberenji-stack/discipline-architecture-2-referee"
license: "Apache-2.0"
keywords:
  - reproducibility
  - language models
  - computational mathematics
  - verification
  - research methodology
  - Lean 4
  - formal verification
abstract: >-
  A dependency-free Python suite that separates the party computing a result
  from the party announcing whether it came out. A runtime referee refuses
  typed verdicts, requires a declaration before every verdict and a negative
  control before the run counts; twelve static checks enforce predictions
  before runs, rows behind every count, guarded domains, propagated
  withdrawals, one definition per word, no forced quantity reported as a
  measurement, resolvable citations, ground-up construction, checkable copies
  of primitives, a gap-free correction ledger, and size-capped working
  documents; a baseline makes new rows visible over a standing backlog; a
  results index makes retrieval a search rather than a memory. A Lean
  referee classes each theorem the kernel accepts by what its proof actually
  reaches (axioms, stand-ins, unbuilt or refuted hypotheses, restatements,
  definitional proofs), eight static checks read Python runs without
  executing them, and a kernel census audits the referee against the list
  of everything the kernel accepted.
